  "id": "DrugBank_DB00974 - MONDO_2319",
  "verbalization": "Edetic Acid is implicated in the pathogenesis of Phosphorus Metabolism Disease through a series of indirect pathways, all centered on its role in the treatment of Hypercalcemia Disease. The evidence suggests that Edetic Acid may modulate calcium homeostasis, leading to reduced calcium levels, which in turn could influence phosphorus metabolism. This is supported by the fact that Edetic Acid is indicated for Hypercalcemia Disease, and its use is associated with the administration of calcium salts such as Calcium Lactate, Calcium Acetate, and Calcium Glycerophosphate, all of which are used to manage hypercalcemia. These calcium salts are known to affect phosphorus metabolism by altering the availability of calcium in the body, potentially influencing the excretion of phosphorus. The integrated evidence suggests that Edetic Acid may exert a mechanistic effect on phosphorus metabolism through its influence on calcium homeostasis, though the exact mechanism remains to be fully elucidated. The clinical relevance of this relationship is supported by the observed association between Edetic Acid and Phosphorus Metabolism Disease, though further studies are needed to confirm the direct causal link.",
